1
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:04,569
This is SWIPEFIT.

2
00:00:04,569 --> 00:00:09,473
Every card you are about to see is a real garment, rendered onto a real person's body.

3
00:00:09,473 --> 00:00:12,667
And one skin scan decides which garments you see at all.

4
00:00:12,667 --> 00:00:15,587
Online, you buy clothes on a model who is not you.

5
00:00:15,587 --> 00:00:20,026
The colour that suits them may fight you, and you find out after it arrives.

6
00:00:20,026 --> 00:00:23,064
SWIPEFIT moves that decision to before the purchase.

7
00:00:23,064 --> 00:00:27,839
First, the one thing a skin scan cannot tell us: what you actually want to wear.

8
00:00:27,839 --> 00:00:32,197
It is asked once, and it filters the catalogue rather than profiling you.

9
00:00:32,197 --> 00:00:33,701
The scan needs two photos,

10
00:00:33,701 --> 00:00:38,274
because a face reading and a full-body try-on want genuinely different framing.

11
00:00:38,274 --> 00:00:40,010
On a real phone you take them.

12
00:00:40,010 --> 00:00:42,730
Here, three bundled people span the tone range.

13
00:00:42,730 --> 00:00:45,361
I will use the deep-tone person deliberately.

14
00:00:45,361 --> 00:00:47,933
Most colour tools misread deep skin as cool.

15
00:00:47,933 --> 00:00:50,563
This one does not, and I want that on camera.

16
00:00:50,563 --> 00:00:53,823
This is a live call to YouCam Skin AI, happening now.

17
00:00:53,823 --> 00:00:58,189
Nothing here is pre-baked — the network request is genuinely in flight.

18
00:00:58,189 --> 00:00:59,142
Warm, and deep.

19
00:00:59,142 --> 00:00:59,904
Deep Autumn.

20
00:00:59,904 --> 00:01:03,461
The API returns colour only — no undertone field exists.

21
00:01:03,461 --> 00:01:06,827
Every warm-cool judgement is derived here, in CIELAB,

22
00:01:06,827 --> 00:01:09,939
against a neutral axis that moves with lightness.

23
00:01:09,939 --> 00:01:13,623
That curve is exactly why this reads warm instead of cool.

24
00:01:13,623 --> 00:01:17,679
Sixty pieces from nine real brands, re-sorted around that one reading.

25
00:01:17,679 --> 00:01:19,185
Not filtered — re-ordered.

26
00:01:19,185 --> 00:01:23,820
A shopper who only ever saw flattering colours could not tell the sort was doing

27
00:01:23,820 --> 00:01:24,342
anything.

28
00:01:24,342 --> 00:01:28,237
Tap the card and it tells you why it scored what it scored, in words.

29
00:01:28,237 --> 00:01:32,075
Terracotta runs warm, the same direction as this person's undertone.

30
00:01:32,075 --> 00:01:33,458
And this is the claim.

31
00:01:33,458 --> 00:01:38,300
That garment, rendered onto that body, through YouCam Apparel Virtual Try-On.

32
00:01:38,300 --> 00:01:40,375
Not a stock photo, not a mock-up.

33
00:01:40,375 --> 00:01:41,872
Notice the brand is hidden.

34
00:01:41,872 --> 00:01:44,976
Most people buy the label before they judge the garment,

35
00:01:44,976 --> 00:01:47,526
so SWIPEFIT hides it until after the decision.

36
00:01:47,526 --> 00:01:50,742
That single choice is what makes the next number possible.

37
00:01:50,742 --> 00:01:54,125
Swipe right, and only then does the brand reveal itself.

38
00:01:54,125 --> 00:01:56,542
The bag fills, and the deck counts down.

39
00:01:56,542 --> 00:02:00,219
Swipe left on something that fights the reading, and it is gone.

40
00:02:00,219 --> 00:02:03,608
The sort is doing real work, not decorating a random order.

41
00:02:03,608 --> 00:02:06,465
Now I turn the label back on, and keep swiping.

42
00:02:06,465 --> 00:02:10,659
That gives the console the two halves it needs: decisions made blind,

43
00:02:10,659 --> 00:02:12,908
and decisions made knowing the brand.

44
00:02:12,908 --> 00:02:16,646
The bag spans brands, because a shopper does not think in shops.

45
00:02:16,646 --> 00:02:19,742
Each brand keeps its own colour across the whole app.

46
00:02:19,742 --> 00:02:22,266
Now the part the API cannot do on its own.

47
00:02:22,266 --> 00:02:25,512
It takes one garment per call — so SWIPEFIT chains it.

48
00:02:25,512 --> 00:02:28,817
The rendered top becomes the body the trousers go onto,

49
00:02:28,817 --> 00:02:31,342
and you get a complete outfit on yourself.

50
00:02:31,342 --> 00:02:34,411
There is no payment here, and there is not meant to be.

51
00:02:34,411 --> 00:02:37,816
SWIPEFIT opens the brand's own product page and takes no cut.

52
00:02:37,816 --> 00:02:41,108
That is what makes brands partners rather than competitors.

53
00:02:41,108 --> 00:02:45,713
Every figure on this screen is measured from the swipes I just made on this device.

54
00:02:45,713 --> 00:02:48,542
Nothing is simulated, and nothing is a placeholder.

55
00:02:48,542 --> 00:02:52,045
And this is the measurement no retailer can run on their own shop.

56
00:02:52,045 --> 00:02:55,495
Keep rate with the label hidden, against keep rate with it shown.

57
00:02:55,495 --> 00:02:59,688
In an ordinary store the shopper can always see whose product they are holding,

58
00:02:59,688 --> 00:03:02,129
so brand pull and garment appeal arrive fused.

59
00:03:02,129 --> 00:03:06,375
Hide the label, and the gap between those two numbers is what the name is worth.

60
00:03:06,375 --> 00:03:06,927
SWIPEFIT.

61
00:03:06,927 --> 00:03:08,828
The face decides what you wear.

62
00:03:08,828 --> 00:03:11,956
Built on YouCam Skin AI and Apparel Virtual Try-On,

63
00:03:11,956 --> 00:03:15,635
with the colour science implemented rather than gestured at.

64
00:03:15,635 --> 00:03:17,475
Thanks very much for watching.
